<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Chronicle of the Morea</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">14th century</span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Chronicle of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Morea</a> is a fourteenth-century history of the Frankish Principality of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaea">Achaea</a> in the Peloponnese, surviving in Greek, French, Aragonese and Italian versions. It covers the conquest after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a>, the reigns of the Villehardouin princes and the later history of the principality. It is a main source for Frankish <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> and its baronies and for local peoples such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/melingoi">Melingoi</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/chronicle-of-the-morea.jpg" alt="Chronicle of the Morea" width="320" height="237" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Text_from_the_Chronicle_of_Morea.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>The Chronicle runs to more than 9,000 lines and tells how the crusaders who settled in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a> after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fourth-crusade">Fourth Crusade</a> established feudalism there, covering the years from 1204 to 1292 or later, depending on the version. It opens with a prologue of 1302 verses, and its account of the administrative laws and practices of the Principality of Achaia, and of life in a feudal community, makes it a major source for the Frankish period in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a> despite its historical inaccuracies.</p><p>The original text is lost, and there is no agreed view on whether the Greek or the French version came first. The Greek is the only verse version, in unrhymed fifteen-syllable political verse in the spoken Greek of the time, with French loanwords. It survives in two parallel texts in five <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manuscript">manuscripts</a>: the older <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a> text, of 9219 verses, which runs to 1292, and a later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> text that is plainer in language and drops several remarks hostile to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>. The French prose text reaches 1304, the Italian is a later summary with several mistakes, and the Aragonese was compiled in 1393 for Jean Fernandez de Heredia, Grand Master of the Knights of St. John.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The author appears to have been a Frank or a gasmoule, the child of a mixed French and Greek marriage. He admired the crusaders and showed contempt for the local population and for the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-empire">Roman Empire</a>, though he still called the Byzantine Greeks Romans. According to the scholar Polet, this hostility is why the work did not pass into popular culture once the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> had left the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>The Chronicle's language reflects the rapid change from medieval to modern Greek, and the work is counted, with the Ptochoprodromic poems and the acritic songs, among the beginnings of modern Greek literature. J. A. Buchon printed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> text in 1840 and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copenhagen">Copenhagen</a> text in 1845, John Schmitt set the two side by side in 1889, and Harold E. Lurier published a translation of the Greek text in 1964.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “The Chronicle of Morea” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1205 they conquered the western Peloponnese, defeating a much larger Greek force at the Olive Grove of Koundouros in Messenia with a small army that the <b>Chronicle of the Morea</b> puts at about 700 men.</blockquote><a href="/empire/principality-of-achaea">Principality of Achaea · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>After the Rosieres line died out, the inheritance of Margaret of Passavant to Akova was disputed in the 1270s, a famous case recorded in the <b>Chronicle of the Morea</b> and in the Assizes of Romania, and much of the barony passed to the princely domain.</blockquote><a href="/state/barony-of-akova">Barony of Akova</a></li><li><blockquote>Beyond these facts, recorded in the <b>Chronicle of the Morea</b> and Angevin documents, little is known of the barony's internal life.</blockquote><a href="/state/barony-of-chalandritsa">Barony of Chalandritsa</a></li><li><blockquote>He was the model knight of the <b>Chronicle of the Morea</b>, admired for his courage despite rebelling against Prince William II and eloping with a vassal's wife.</blockquote><a href="/state/barony-of-karytaina">Barony of Karytaina</a></li><li><blockquote>Little detail of daily government survives beyond mentions in the <b>Chronicle of the Morea</b> and Angevin records.</blockquote><a href="/state/barony-of-vostitsa">Barony of Vostitsa</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Chronicle of the Morea</b> shows them still distinct in the 13th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/melingoi">Melingoi</a></li></ul></section>
